 "script": [
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "In eighteen seventy-six, it rained meat. Not cats and dogs. Actual meat."
  },
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "And no, I'm not kidding. They called it the Kentucky Meat Shower."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "Here's the deal. March third, on a farm in Bath County, a woman named Mrs. Crouch is outside, making soap."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "The sky is totally clear. And then, flakes start falling. Like snow. But red."
  },
  {
   "beat": "rain",
   "text": "Chunks of meat, some as big as your palm, splat all over the yard and the fence."
  },
  {
   "beat": "rain",
   "text": "A strip about a hundred yards long, just covered in meat."
  },
  {
   "beat": "taste",
   "text": "And you know what two local guys did? They tasted it."
  },
  {
   "beat": "taste",
   "text": "One said it was mutton. The other said deer."
  },
  {
   "beat": "lab",
   "text": "Scientists got samples. Some found lung, some found muscle, some found cartilage."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "So where did it come from? A cloud made of meat? Wrong."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "The best guess is a flock of vultures. When vultures get spooked, they throw up, so they're lighter and can fly away faster."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "So that day, a bunch of birds lost their lunch, all over one Kentucky farm."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "So next time you stare up at a clear blue sky, maybe keep your mouth shut."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "That's real. That's about it, folks. Follow for more strange history. See ya!"
  }
 ],
 "science": [
  {
   "claim": "On 3 March 1876, near Rankin in Bath County, Kentucky, flakes and chunks of what looked like meat fell from a clear sky over a strip roughly 100 yards long and 50 wide, while Mrs. Allen Crouch was making soap in her yard; pieces were reported up to about 3-4 inches across; two men tasted it and guessed mutton or venison; samples examined later were identified by different people as lung, muscle and cartilage; the leading explanation is a flock of vultures disgorging (vultures vomit to lighten themselves when startled).",
   "working": "",
   "source": "Wikipedia, Kentucky meat shower; Smithsonian; Scientific American (1876); New York Times (10 March 1876)"
  }
 ],
